1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,172
In this walkthrough, we'll fix a small bug
with Copilot in Visual Studio Code.

2
00:00:05,352 --> 00:00:09,789
Then a teammate will review the pull
request using the GitHub command line.

3
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:14,145
Start with issue seventeen.

4
00:00:14,325 --> 00:00:18,913
The validator accepts zero, even though an
order needs at least one item.

5
00:00:19,093 --> 00:00:20,845
Keep the function signature.

6
00:00:21,025 --> 00:00:22,669
One and five should pass.

7
00:00:22,849 --> 00:00:25,197
Zero and negative one should fail.

8
00:00:28,350 --> 00:00:30,461
In VS Code, select Plan.

9
00:00:30,641 --> 00:00:34,043
Give Copilot the issue, the validator, and
its tests.

10
00:00:34,223 --> 00:00:37,786
Ask where the bug is and what regression
test would expose it.

11
00:00:37,966 --> 00:00:40,785
Check the plan against the files before
moving on.

12
00:00:46,350 --> 00:00:49,667
Switch to Agent and ask for just the
regression test.

13
00:00:49,847 --> 00:00:54,231
Review the proposed test command and run
it in the approved environment.

14
00:00:54,411 --> 00:00:57,878
The new test should fail because zero
still returns true.

15
00:00:58,058 --> 00:00:59,704
That's the bug we're fixing.

16
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:08,987
Now ask Agent to change the comparison.

17
00:01:09,167 --> 00:01:11,983
Review the edit, then rerun the same
tests.

18
00:01:12,163 --> 00:01:14,352
The zero case should pass this time.

19
00:01:14,532 --> 00:01:18,360
The tests for one, five, and negative one
should still pass.

20
00:01:23,350 --> 00:01:24,932
Open Source Control.

21
00:01:25,112 --> 00:01:26,691
Read the diff yourself.

22
00:01:26,871 --> 00:01:30,551
There should be one changed comparison and
one added test.

23
00:01:30,731 --> 00:01:33,791
Commit both files and publish the feature
branch.

24
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:39,592
From the authorized terminal, open the
pull request.

25
00:01:39,772 --> 00:01:44,133
Link issue seventeen and include the test
result in the description.

26
00:01:44,313 --> 00:01:46,971
The example creates pull request forty
two.

27
00:01:47,151 --> 00:01:49,021
Hand that number to the reviewer.

28
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:54,740
Now you're the reviewer.

29
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:57,803
Use gh pr view to read the description.

30
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:01,587
Next, run gh pr diff.

31
00:02:01,767 --> 00:02:04,629
Check the one line fix and the test for
zero.

32
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:08,303
Use gh pr checks.

33
00:02:08,483 --> 00:02:12,129
Wait for required checks to finish, and
confirm they pass.

34
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:16,843
Once you've checked the diff and the
evidence, use approve to record your

35
00:02:16,843 --> 00:02:17,347
decision.

36
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:23,808
If the change needs work, request changes
and tell the author what to fix.

37
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:29,521
Try the same workflow in the training
repository.

38
00:02:29,701 --> 00:02:32,917
Show the failing test, the fix, and the
passing test.

39
00:02:33,097 --> 00:02:37,332
Then ask a teammate to review the pull
request with GitHub CLI.
